🧾 Oxide / Omicron Debug & SSH Tunnel Command Summary
🔎 1. Check what’s running on the Omicron/Nexus host (illumos)
svcs | grep -i omicron

Shows Omicron-related SMF services (baseline, nexus, etc.)

ps -ef | grep -i nexus
ps -ef | grep -i omicron

Confirms Nexus and related control-plane processes are running.

zoneadm list -cv

Lists all zones (Nexus, CockroachDB, Crucible, DNS, etc.) and helps identify where services live.

netstat -an -f inet | grep LISTEN

Shows listening ports (used to discover internal service endpoints like 12220/12228/80).

🌐 2. Inspect service endpoints
ipadm show-addr

Displays IPs (important: internal IPv6 + 172.30.x control network used by Omicron services).

netstat -an | grep LISTEN

Used inside zones to identify which services bind to which ports.

🔌 3. SSH tunnel setup (core step)
❌ Wrong target (internal service layer)
ssh -L 12220:[fd00:1122:3344:101::c]:12228 user@host

Tunneled to backend Nexus/auth service → partial API only.

✔ Correct target (API gateway layer)
ssh -L 12220:172.30.2.7:80 michael@192.168.1.21

Forwards Windows localhost → Omicron API gateway (required for CLI login).

🧪 4. Test tunnel from Windows
Test-NetConnection 127.0.0.1 -Port 12220

Checks if SSH tunnel is actually reachable.

curl.exe -v http://127.0.0.1:12220/

Basic HTTP test (must NOT reset connection).

curl.exe -v http://127.0.0.1:12220/device/verify

Checks whether full OAuth device flow endpoint exists (only works on gateway layer).

🔐 5. CLI login flow (correct method)
oxide --insecure auth login --host http://127.0.0.1:12220

Starts device-code authentication flow.

Output:

opens /device/verify
provides login code
completes token exchange automatically
🧪 6. Manual API exploration (what we tried, but not recommended)
curl -v http://127.0.0.1:12220/device/auth

Raw auth endpoint (requires API version + form encoding).

curl -v -H "api-version: 1.0.0" ...

Discovered strict API versioning requirements.

curl -v -X POST -H "Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded" ...

Required for backend auth endpoint, but still not full CLI flow.

⚠️ Key failures we hit (important learning signals)
Connection reset by peer → wrong service layer
os error 10054 → backend rejecting CLI auth flow
missing api-version → internal API requirement
missing client_id → raw auth endpoint requirement
/device/verify reset → endpoint not exposed on backend layer
🧠 Final mental model
12228 → internal auth backend ❌ (not CLI-facing)
172.30.2.7:80 → API gateway ✔ (correct for oxide login)
oxide CLI → ONLY works against gateway layer